The villagers all envied the household with the golden-egg goose, though only its owner knew the careful work of patience.

A young farmhand at the village kept a chalk tally on a beam in the barn — one mark for each egg the goose laid, one cross for each coin paid at the market. The goose laid 2 golden eggs a day, and at the market each egg fetched 9 coins. By the time the small chest of coins beside the hearth held 198 coins in all, the boy stood and stared up at the marks, while Diana sat by the door of the barn, pleased with her small fortune, listening to the goose ruffle her feathers.

Question: for how many days the goose had been laying?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Daily revenue is per-day eggs × per-egg coins; days = total-coins / coins-per-day.

{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [per-day 2 per-egg-coins 9 total-coins 198 coins-per-day (* per-day per-egg-coins)] (quot total-coins coins-per-day))"}}]}
<|end|>


