Greed and patience, as everyone knows, do not sit at the same table — and a golden-egg goose tests them both.

More than once Edward had heard a sly voice whisper that the goose was wasted on slow daily laying. But he swallowed the thought each time, fed the bird, and let the gold come at its own pace. The goose laid 1 golden egg a day for 29 days, and when the eggs were finally carried in their basket to the market they fetched 493 coins in all — he sat by the hearth, content in her quiet life, and looked at the worn ledger where every egg, every coin, every trip to the market had been written down in a careful hand.

Write a Clojure expression that computes the price in coins at which each single egg had sold.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Total eggs is per-day × days; per-egg price = total-coins / total-eggs.

{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [per-day 1 days 29 total-coins 493 total-eggs (* per-day days)] (quot total-coins total-eggs))"}}]}
<|end|>


