A farmer once kept a goose who laid a golden egg every morning, plain and ordinary in every other way.

A grain merchant from the next valley had offered to buy Morgan's eggs in advance, but only at a price tied to the goose's typical daily yield. Morgan pulled out the week's notes — 3, 4, 2 over 3 days — and set about computing the rounded-down average, imagining all that he might gain at the thought of what a fair number would be worth.

Write a Clojure expression that computes the average eggs per day, rounded down to a whole number.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
I sum the yields, divide by the count of days, and take the integer quotient.

{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [daily-yields [3 4 2] total (apply + daily-yields) days (count daily-yields)] (quot total days))"}}]}
<|end|>


