Vix the fox crept beneath an orchard vine, his stomach hollow with hunger, and stopped before a heavy cluster of purple grapes. She rose up on her hind legs, nose lifted, the tips of her ears just brushing the lowest leaves at 3 feet above the dust. From that stretch she judged she could spring another 3 feet cleanly into the air. The grapes shone in the late sun, dusty-bloomed and impossibly round, and Vix measured the height between her paws and the prize.

