The Crow knew that water in the world is sometimes hidden where only the patient can reach it.

The afternoon sun beat down hard, and Korvus, with a thirst that hurt to bear, settled at the edge of an orchard where a stout jar had been left out. The water inside lay 6 centimeters from the bottom — far below the 16 centimeters mark he needed to drink. Beneath a nearby tree he found a small heap of pebbles. He weighed one in his beak: it would lift the surface by exactly 1 centimeter. Now it was only a matter of working out how many he would have to drop.

Question: the smallest number of pebbles Korvus needs to drop in to reach the target water level?
