In a farmer's quiet yard, where ripe apples hung from a low branch above the dust, Renard had been working himself into a fine sweat. He had sworn — out loud, to the indifferent hens — that 6 attempts were all he would grant the apples; after that, he would name them underripe and slink off with what dignity remained. He had already tried 5 times, each spring shorter than the last, and stood shouting until her voice cracked beneath the lowest apple, deciding.

