Rex the dog had hoped to chew through 5 marrow-bones all afternoon, the way he had planned it that morning, lined up neatly in a row by the fire. But on the way back across the stream he growled at his own reflection — imagining all that he might gain — and a single marrow-bone tumbled in. By dusk, as he laid out the row, regretting every careless step, the line was shorter than it should have been. The difference between the imagined row and the real one was the day's small lesson.

