At the edge of a country lane stood a high-sided wooden cart, and over its rim spilled long blackberry canes a passing child had laid there to dry. The darkest berry hung 6 feet above the rutted road. Renard, dreaming of the next meal, circled the cart twice. He tested his jump and found it good for 1 foot of clean lift. He settled into the dust to count exactly how many such springs, each adding cleanly to the last, would carry him to the berry.

Question: the smallest number of leaps Renard needs to reach the fruit?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [grape-height 6 per-jump 1] (quot (+ grape-height (dec per-jump)) per-jump))"}}]}
