Greed and patience, as everyone knows, do not sit at the same table — and a golden-egg goose tests them both.

A young farmhand at the village liked to come round at sunrise just to watch Carol collect from the goose. The bird would settle, ruffle, and stand, leaving 3 golden eggs in the straw — never more, never fewer. Carol would gather them pleased with her small fortune, smiling at the boy, and tuck them into a folded cloth. She kept up the routine for 7 days, and at the end the boy asked, eyes wide, just how many golden eggs the goose had given altogether.

Write a Clojure expression that computes how many eggs the goose laid in total over those 7 days.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here's the calculation.

{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [per-day 3 days 7] (* per-day days))"}}]}
<|end|>


