The Lion stalked the field where the Bulls had grazed in peace for many seasons. All this took place near the garden.

At dusk on a flat plain, the silhouettes of 5 bulls stood widely scattered, each carrying the same well-known strength 11. Roar the lion, of strength 11, watched the long shadows lean across the grass saying very little. The lion could meet any one of those silhouettes alone; the herd's combined strength of 55, however, would simply trample him. The bulls themselves shifted in a panic at every distant snap of grass. All that remained was the boolean: did the lion truly need them divided to win?

Question: whether Roar can defeat each bull alone (lion-strength > bull-strength) yet would lose against the herd combined (lion-strength < combined-strength)?
