Strength shared is strength multiplied — a truth the Lion knew well, and worked patiently to undo. It happened along the road.

In the valley grassland, Roar the lion crouched at the reed-line stepping deliberately, watching 3 bulls drink at separate bends of the slow stream. Each bull had strength 14; the lion's own strength was 14. Solo, no bull at the water could withstand him; together at the bend they had once held 42 between them, more than enough to chase a lion off. The math of the situation was clear, and the only thing left to settle was the boolean: did the lion's chance depend on keeping the bulls divided?

Question: whether Roar can defeat each bull alone (lion-strength > bull-strength) yet would lose against the herd combined (lion-strength < combined-strength)?
