Strength shared is strength multiplied — a truth the Lion knew well, and worked patiently to undo. All this took place near the meadow.

In a wide clearing rimmed by tall grass, 3 bulls stood at suspicious distances, each of strength 2. Roar the lion crossed the clearing stepping deliberately, knowing his own strength to be 11. Single-handedly against a single bull, the lion held the upper paw; but if the herd closed ranks the combined strength of 6 would have made short work of him. The lion sat in the long shadow at the edge and considered: did victory depend strictly on keeping them apart?

Question: whether Roar can defeat each bull alone (lion-strength > bull-strength) yet would lose against the herd combined (lion-strength < combined-strength)?
