Greed and patience, as everyone knows, do not sit at the same table — and a golden-egg goose tests them both.

In the village the rumor went that Oliver's goose laid silver as well as golden eggs, but he only smiled and said nothing of the kind. The goose laid 1 golden egg a day, and at the market each egg fetched 40 coins. By the time the small chest of coins beside the hearth held 520 coins in all, every morning he would carry the eggs to the market folded inside a cloth, his eyes greedy with want at the soft weight against his hip, listening to the coins jingle home each evening.

Write a Clojure expression that computes for how many days the goose had been laying.
<|end|>
