Each evening at the village, Morgan sat at a creaking table and laid out the day's haul: 3 golden eggs from the goose, warm and shining like little suns. They kept a chalk mark on a beam for every egg, his thoughts already on more, watching the row of marks lengthen. After 22 days of marks, the beam was nearly full, and Morgan squinted up at it trying to count exactly how many eggs in all the goose had laid.

Write a Clojure expression that computes how many eggs the goose laid in total over those 22 days.
