Greed has cost more than one creature what they already had.

The butcher had tied up 10 scraps of meat in a paper for Rex the dog to carry home, and the count had been said aloud, both of them nodding. On the footbridge over the river the paper sagged near the water; he saw a phantom dog with a richer parcel and, his mouth watering at the thought of more, snapped — losing one scrap to the river. By evening, when the cook checked the parcel against the butcher's tally, the actual count fell short of the expected by exactly that single greedy moment.

Question: how many scraps of meat Rex fell short by, compared to what he expected to bring home?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
I subtract the actual count (one fewer) from the expected count to find the gap.

{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [expected-bones 10 actual-bones (dec expected-bones)] (- expected-bones actual-bones))"}}]}
<|end|>


