A hungry Fox came upon a vine of grapes hanging just out of reach.

In the corner of an orchard Renard the fox stopped beneath a low arbor where a heavy bunch of purple grapes hung 10 feet above the trodden grass. Renard, weak with hunger, paused there with his mouth watering at the dusty bloom on each grape. A trial spring carried him 2 feet into the air, and he reasoned that each leap, if he could somehow build upon the last, would bring the prize 2 feet closer.

Question: the smallest number of leaps Renard needs to reach the fruit?
