The Lion stalked the field where the Bulls had grazed in peace for many seasons.

On a high hilltop pasture, where the wind carried the smell of broken grass for miles, the 5 bulls had once been impossible to approach. Now Roar the lion crossed the bald slope openly. Over the long, slow campaign, he had brought down 3 of them, never two on the same week. The remaining bulls watched from far ridges, shouting until her voice cracked, no longer trusting the wind. He paused untroubled by what others thought on a flat stone and reckoned how many of the herd were still standing.

