Greed and patience, as everyone knows, do not sit at the same table — and a golden-egg goose tests them both. It happened by the farm.

More than once Alice had heard a sly voice whisper that the goose was wasted on slow daily laying. But she swallowed the thought each time, fed the bird, and let the gold come at its own pace. The goose laid 1 golden egg a day for 15 days, and when the eggs were finally carried in their basket to the market they fetched 525 coins in all — she sat by the hearth, content in her quiet life, and looked at the worn ledger where every egg, every coin, every trip to the market had been written down in a careful hand.

