There was once an extraordinary goose whose every morning gift was a single egg of pure gold. All this took place at the market.

A young farmhand at the market kept a chalk tally on a beam in the barn — one mark for each egg the goose laid, one cross for each coin paid at the market. The goose laid 1 golden egg a day, and at the market each egg fetched 11 coins. By the time the small chest of coins beside the hearth held 132 coins in all, the boy stood and stared up at the marks, while Carol sat by the door of the barn, content in her quiet life, listening to the goose ruffle her feathers.

Write a Clojure expression that computes for how many days the goose had been laying.
