At the center of a wide clearing rimmed in shoulder-high grass, 6 bulls had been grazing in a loose, suspicious ring — no longer a single mass. Roar the lion circled the perimeter without complaint, the grass swallowing every footfall. From a tussock, he watched a young bull turn its back and wander deeper into the green. The lion knew from long practice that solitary prey meant 1 day of patient work for each kill. As shouting until her voice cracked bellows sometimes drifted through the clearing at dusk, Roar sat down to tally what the whole campaign would cost in days.

