Bell the dog had hoped to chew through 11 marrow-bones all afternoon, the way she had planned it that morning, lined up neatly in a row by the fire. But on the way back across the stream she growled at her own reflection — imagining all that he might gain — and a single marrow-bone tumbled in. By dusk, as she laid out the row, wondering how it had come to this, the line was shorter than it should have been. The difference between the imagined row and the real one was the day's small lesson.

Write a Clojure expression that computes how many marrow-bones Bell fell short by, compared to what she expected to bring home.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [expected-bones 11 actual-bones (dec expected-bones)] (- expected-bones actual-bones))"}}]}
<|end|>


