At dusk by a farmhouse stoop, Korvus the crow paused beside a chipped pitcher. The water inside, only 3 centimeters deep, sent up a thin reflection of the sky. He was dry-throated and desperate, and so he did what every clever crow knows to do: he gathered 11 beads from the gravel along the path and dropped them in, one after another. Each bead shouldered the water up by 2 centimeters, and he kept count.

Question: the new water level in the pitcher (in centimeters) after all 11 beads have been dropped in?
