Strength shared is strength multiplied — a truth the Lion knew well, and worked patiently to undo.

In a wide clearing rimmed by tall grass, 5 bulls stood at suspicious distances, each of strength 9. Roar the lion crossed the clearing with eyes always on the path, knowing his own strength to be 10. Single-handedly against a single bull, the lion held the upper paw; but if the herd closed ranks the combined strength of 45 would have made short work of him. The lion sat in the long shadow at the edge and considered: did victory depend strictly on keeping them apart?

Write a Clojure expression that computes whether Roar can defeat each bull alone (lion-strength > bull-strength) yet would lose against the herd combined (lion-strength < combined-strength).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
I check both: lion-strength > one bull's strength (alone wins) AND lion-strength < combined-strength (together the herd wins).

{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [lion-strength 10 bulls 5 bull-strength 9 combined-strength (* bulls bull-strength)] (and (> lion-strength bull-strength) (< lion-strength combined-strength)))"}}]}
<|end|>


