Along the edge of a sunbaked vineyard, an old fig tree had grown over the top of a low stone wall, dropping its fruit on the wrong side. Renard, wishing she had been more careful of having strayed so far for a meal, padded to the foot of the wall. He reared up: the tips of his ears reached 2 feet above the gravel, and experience told him he could spring no more than 3 feet above that. The figs hung there, lazy and purple. He stared and measured.

