In the valley grassland, Roar the lion crouched at the reed-line with eyes always on the path, watching 5 bulls drink at separate bends of the slow stream. Each bull had strength 13; the lion's own strength was 13. Solo, no bull at the water could withstand him; together at the bend they had once held 65 between them, more than enough to chase a lion off. The math of the situation was clear, and the only thing left to settle was the boolean: did the lion's chance depend on keeping the bulls divided?

