The afternoon was hot enough to crack a leaf, and Caw, dry-throated and desperate, settled at the rim of an old pitcher that someone had left in the sun. The water at the bottom — only 5 centimeters deep — shimmered just out of reach. A pile of dry pebbles sat nearby. One by one she ferried them up and let them fall, 9 pebbles altogether, each one shouldering the surface up by another 1 centimeter.

