A small crowd of finches watched as Caw alighted shouting until her voice cracked beside a tall jar. She could see the dark shimmer of water sitting 6 centimeters from the bottom — well short of the 15 centimeters she required. By the foot of the jar lay a scatter of dry beads. She dropped one in: a soft plink, and the water climbed by 2 centimeters. The finches leaned closer. She began the steady work of figuring exactly how many more would do the trick.

Question: the smallest number of beads Caw needs to drop in to reach the target water level?
