Three Bulls had grazed and fought together for so long that no Lion dared trouble them.

At dusk on a flat plain, the silhouettes of 4 bulls stood widely scattered, each carrying the same well-known strength 6. Mane the lion, of strength 8, watched the long shadows lean across the grass with steady, careful steps. The lion could meet any one of those silhouettes alone; the herd's combined strength of 24, however, would simply trample her. The bulls themselves shifted shouting until her voice cracked at every distant snap of grass. All that remained was the boolean: did the lion truly need them divided to win?

Write a Clojure expression that computes whether Mane can defeat each bull alone (lion-strength > bull-strength) yet would lose against the herd combined (lion-strength < combined-strength).
<|end|>
<|asst|>
{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [lion-strength 8 bulls 4 bull-strength 6 combined-strength (* bulls bull-strength)] (and (> lion-strength bull-strength) (< lion-strength combined-strength)))"}}]}
<|end|>


