The villagers all envied the household with the golden-egg goose, though only its owner knew the careful work of patience.

In the farm the rumor went that Henry's goose laid silver as well as golden eggs, but he only smiled and said nothing of the kind. The goose laid 1 golden egg a day for 16 days, and when the eggs were finally carried in their basket to the market they fetched 400 coins in all — every morning he would carry the eggs to the market folded inside a cloth, his mouth watering at the thought of more at the soft weight against his hip, listening to the coins jingle home each evening.

Question: the price in coins at which each single egg had sold?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [per-day 1 days 16 total-coins 400 total-eggs (* per-day days)] (quot total-coins total-eggs))"}}]}
<|end|>


