It is the way of the Ant to gather, and the way of the Grasshopper to play.

On the eve of the first spring thaw — at the end of 58 days of winter — the two neighbors sat very differently. In a basket at the heart of the burrow, Tic had begun winter with 224 crumbs, and across the cold weeks he had taken only 2 crumbs a day. Chirp, in his thin bag, had begun with only 29 crumbs, and ate 1 crumb per day, weak with hunger. A stockpile, once it runs out, cannot go below zero.

Write a Clojure expression that computes how many crumbs Tic has left at the end of winter.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
{"tool_calls":[{"name":"eval","args":{"form":"(let [ant-stock 224 ant-rate 2 gh-stock 29 gh-rate 1 days 58 ant-left (- ant-stock (* ant-rate days)) gh-left (- gh-stock (* gh-rate days))] (max ant-left 0))"}}]}
<|end|>


